115-## Known gaps / follow-ups
116-- `regex` follows the crate's linear-time engine: there are no backreferences or
117-  look-around, by design.
118-- The module does not expose every match of a search (only the first, plus
119-  replacement/split which visit all matches). A future `regex.find_all` would be
120-  a natural addition, not a defect in this phase.
251:The four gates were run first, against the tree the previous rounds left. `cargo
252-fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings` and `cargo test
253---all-targets` were already green. Two things were not finished:
254-
255-| # | Severity | What was wrong | What changed |
256-|---|---|---|---|
257-| 1 | MAJOR | The phase's definition of done asks for "each a **caught** `RuntimeError` naming the function, never a panic". `edge_a_module_function_refuses_bad_arguments_by_name` proved the *refusal* — non-zero exit, the function named in stderr, no `panicked` — which is the process-level half. Nothing proved the other half: that the refusal is something a program can catch and carry on from. A builtin that unwound instead of raising would have passed it. | `edge_a_bad_module_call_is_a_catchable_error_and_not_an_abort` runs three of the same bad calls — `text.uppercase()` (no arguments), `list.map(…, 5)` (too many), `files.read(1)` (wrong type) — inside `try … catch error`, and asserts stdout is exactly `caught\nafter\n`. `after` is on stdout only if the statement past the `try` ran, so a fatal refusal fails the assertion even though the program printed something. |
258-| 2 | MAJOR | The same test's sibling — and this is what makes #1 mean something — the unknown-module case is a different kind of failure. `nosuchmodule.read("x")` is an `AnalyzerError`, raised before the program starts, so `catch error` does not catch it. Lumping the two together would have claimed "module errors are catchable" on the strength of a row that is not a runtime error at all. | The new test asserts the boundary directly: the same program with `nosuchmodule.read("x")` inside the `try` exits non-zero and never prints `after`. So the three rows above are a claim about `RuntimeError` specifically, and a future change that made analyzer errors catchable — which would be wrong — fails this. |
